~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
[EXPLAIN] What this step does
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Trains the final predictive model and tests three variants on compounds the model never saw (15%
  of the data is set         aside before anything else happens, so the test is honest):
  Exp A - baseline: a stacking ensemble (several different algorithms whose predictions are
  combined by a final model).         Exp B - A plus feature selection: keeps only the fingerprint
  bits that actually help prediction (permutation         importance: a bit is useful if randomly
  scrambling it makes predictions worse).         Exp C - B plus data augmentation: adds slightly
  noisy copies of the training molecules' potencies (Gaussian noise) to         see whether this
  makes the model more robust.
  Metrics: R2 (1 = perfect, 0 = no better than predicting the mean potency) and MAE (average error
  in pIC50 units;         0.5 means a typical error of ~3-fold in IC50). The 95% CI comes from
  bootstrap resampling of the test set: it shows how         much the score would move if a
  different sample of test molecules had been drawn. With so few test molecules the CI is
  wide, so small R2 differences between experiments are often not meaningful.
  Outputs used downstream: results/04_best_model.joblib (the un-augmented model used for the
  virtual screening in step 05)         and results/04_selected_features_mask.npy (which
  fingerprint bits are kept).
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
